# Local sequential write — storage capability check.
#
# This job exists to answer one question: can fio drive a real I/O job against
# the directory the object-storage clusters sit on? It is NOT a benchmark of
# MinIO or Silo and no result from it may be attributed to either product.
#
# The parameters match the run captured in
# docs/01-repo-versions-and-lab/evidence/storage-tooling.txt:
#
#   io_bytes=67108864  bw=1560380KiB/s  runtime=42ms
#
# i.e. 64 MiB of sequential write, direct=1, libaio at iodepth 8. The captured
# output did not record the block size, so this job cannot reproduce that run
# exactly and does not claim to; bs=1M is chosen here and stated.
#
# WHY THESE EXACT PARAMETERS ARE PINNED HERE
# ------------------------------------------
# fio throughput on this host is not reproducible. Measured figures:
#
#   1,560,380 KiB/s   the run captured in storage-tooling.txt
#     167,183 KiB/s   bind-mount target
#      64,377 KiB/s   container target
#      62,400 KiB/s   this job file, first run  (73.6 MiB/s, 869 ms)
#      60,900 KiB/s   this job file, second run (60.9 MiB/s, 1051 ms)
#
# Two things follow. Across targets the spread is about 25x, so the target
# filesystem dominates the number. And the last two rows are the SAME job file,
# SAME target, run back to back minutes apart, differing by 22% — so the number
# does not even repeat under fixed conditions.
#
# The parameters are therefore frozen rather than tuned. A tuned job file would
# invite the next person to adjust it until the number looks presentable, which
# is how a capability check turns into a fabricated benchmark. Change the job
# only alongside a recorded explanation of what the previous numbers meant.
#
# Size is deliberately small (64 MiB). At ~42 ms runtime this job verifies that
# the tool works end to end. It is not long enough to saturate anything, and it
# is not intended to.

[seqwrite]
ioengine=libaio
direct=1
iodepth=8
rw=write
bs=1M
size=64M
filename_format=/data/$jobname
directory=/data
group_reporting=1

# Strip per-iteration stats so the output stays stable across runs and diffable.
# The aggregate bandwidth line is the whole point of this job.